# `.crew/info.md` — who decides, and what needs permission

> Copy this to `{{RECORDS_DIR}}/info.md` and fill it in. **Every agent reads this file fresh at the
> start of a session and again at every gate** — never from memory of an earlier read. It is the one
> place that says who is available, who decides, and what happens when someone is missing.
>
> Separating this from the skills is deliberate: the skills describe **the process**, this file
> describes **the authority**. That way you can change how much you delegate without editing a
> single skill.

---

## Where the roster lives — and why it is not here

🔴 **This file is versioned and shared. It holds the project's AUTHORITY, never one person's setup.**

Which agents are reachable, which models are configured and who is sitting at the keyboard all differ
per machine and change per week. Committing them means two people fighting over one file every
session — your `crew` is their `solo`, because they never installed codex.

That belongs in **`.crew-kit-config`** at the repository root, which is gitignored. Copy
`.crew-kit-config.example` and fill it in. It declares the roster, the models, and whether you are the
`{{OWNER}}` named below.

| | |
|---|---|
| **This file** — versioned | who decides each gate, the red zone, the critical asset, the approved vendors |
| **`.crew-kit-config`** — yours | which agents this machine reaches, which models, who you are |

### "Owner" here means who DECIDES

Not whoever owns the repository. The two coincide in a project you started and separate in a clone or
a fork, and every gate in this kit means the deciding one: the person you go to before touching the
red zone. An upstream maintainer who owns the repository and decides nothing about your copy is not
the owner in this file.

### The owner named here is the default, not the last word

`{{OWNER}}` below is what a fresh clone inherits, and it is right for the person who set the project
up and wrong for anyone who forked it — every gate would send them to ask someone they have never
met. `.crew-kit-config` may therefore set `OWNER` for **that working copy**, and it wins.

🔴 **An override is declared in every report it affects:** *"the project names {{OWNER}} · this copy
runs under \<name\>"*. Silently redirecting "ask the owner" to yourself removes the gate while
leaving its name in the report, which is worse than not having it.

**The mode is derived, not chosen twice.** It is the smaller of what this file allows and what your
roster can reach. A project that permits `crew` still runs `solo` on a machine with one agent — and
the report must say so.

> Same principle the kit already uses for `settings.json` versus `settings.local.json`: a rule that
> applies to everyone is versioned; what is personal is not.

