## 🔴 What `solo` mode changes — read this before assuming a review happened

In `solo`, **cross-review does not exist.** The kit's default policy is that nobody reviews their own
work, and in solo mode that policy cannot be satisfied by a second agent. Pretending otherwise is
worse than not having it, so the rules become:

| Normally | In solo |
|---|---|
| The other agent reviews the plan (`plan-review`) | **You review your own plan** — but on a separate pass, after writing it out in full, and you **declare in the verdict that it was a self-review** |
| The other agent reviews the code (`codereview`) | You review your own diff, **and you run your host's independent reviewers** — for Claude that means `/code-review` **and** `/security-review`. Those are not the same lens as yours, and in solo they are the only second opinion that exists |
| Delegated executors do the small work | You do all of it. Do not delegate to an executor that is not in the roster |
| `/execute-plan` fans out `implementer` agents | Still available — subagents of the same model are a weaker check than a different model, but they are a real one |

**In every solo report, state the mode.** A reader who does not know it was solo will assume a second
agent signed off, and that assumption is exactly how an unreviewed change ships with the appearance
of having been reviewed.

### What `duo` changes

Everything about review works normally. Delegation does not exist: every task is done by a complex
agent. Skip the `agy`/`copilot` sections of `mcp/README.md` entirely.

