## Gates — who decides what

The process is the same in every mode; **the authority is what you set here.** Change these as trust
grows, without touching a skill.

| Gate | Authority | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Plan approval | **human** | never delegate this by default |
| Starting to code | human | after the plan is approved |
| Routing to a low-risk executor | orchestrator | the human sees it in the report |
| Accepting a delegated diff | orchestrator | after reading the diff, not the report |
| Release verdict (🟢/🟡/🔴) | orchestrator | the human decides what to do with it |
| **`git commit`** | **human orders it, orchestrator runs it** | 🔴 never an executor, in any mode |
| **`git push`** | **human orders it explicitly** | 🔴 an order to commit is not an order to push |
| Adding a dependency | **human** | the licence gate must run first (`compliance`) |
| Touching `{{RED_ZONE}}` / `{{CRITICAL_ASSET}}` | **human** | written authorization, before the work |
| Sending data to a new external service | **human, in writing** | see `compliance` |
| Registering a new agent | **human** | after the `onboard-agent` battery; the human picks the role |

## Models in use

In `.crew-kit-config`, not here — see above.

---

## Reread discipline

🔴 **Read this file again at every gate, not once at the start.** A session can run for hours; the
mode or the roster can change mid-session (an agent stops responding, the human restricts something).
An agent acting on a remembered policy is acting on a policy that may no longer be true — and it will
do so confidently.
